<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luba Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luba Empire was a Central African state in the Upemba region of the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, flourishing from about the sixteenth to the late nineteenth century. It was built around a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> and a network of tributary chiefdoms, with institutions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambudye">Bambudye</a> society preserving its traditions. The Luba model of rule influenced neighbouring states, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luba-empire.jpg" alt="Luba Empire" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lunda_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
